# Desktop signing secrets, staged for `scripts/push_env_to_github.py`.
#
# Copy to `desktop/.env.test` (gitignored by `**/.env.*`), fill in, then:
#
#     python3 scripts/push_env_to_github.py --dry-run   # names only, values hidden
#     python3 scripts/push_env_to_github.py
#
# These reach `desktop-release.yml` only as GitHub secrets — a runner cannot
# read a local dotenv. This file is the staging area, not the source of truth.
# Nothing here belongs in git; the real values live in your keychain, in
# ~/.tauri, and in GitHub.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Updater signing (minisign) — Linux, Windows and macOS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Do these two first. Without them the Linux and Windows jobs build their
# bundles successfully and then fail on the final line, because an absent key
# is indistinguishable from a corrupt one:
#
#     failed to decode secret key: incorrect updater private key password:
#     Missing comment in secret key
#
# The key already exists at ~/.tauri/duct-updater.key — paste its contents
# (single line, it is base64). The password is the passphrase set when it was
# generated. There is no way to recover this key if it is lost: every future
# update must be signed by the same key or installed clients will reject it.
TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY=
TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Developer ID signing + notarization — macOS DMG only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT the App Store certificates. A Mac App Store cert
# (MAC_APP_DISTRIBUTION) cannot sign a DMG: Apple's notary service rejects
# anything not signed with Developer ID, and Gatekeeper trusts an App Store
# build only because Apple re-signed it on delivery. A download from
# getduct.ai has no such provenance.

# These three are filled in for you. After downloading the .cer from the Apple
# Developer portal, run:
#
#     python3 scripts/stage_devid_secrets.py --cer <path>.cer --key <path>.key
#
# It exports the .p12 with the PBE form Apple's tooling can actually read
# (OpenSSL 3's default is AES-256/PBKDF2, which security(1) rejects with a
# "wrong password?" error that is not about the password), base64-encodes it
# unwrapped, reads the identity string out of the certificate so it cannot be
# mistyped, and writes all three here. Fill them by hand only if you are not
# using that script.

# Base64 of the exported .p12 — the workflow pipes this through
# `base64 --decode`, so it must be encoded and unwrapped.
DUCT_DEVID_CERT_P12=

# The password set during the .p12 export.
DUCT_DEVID_CERT_PASSWORD=

# The full identity string, exactly as `security find-identity -v -p codesigning`
# prints it, e.g. "Developer ID Application: Shirish Kadam (LWX9595M5V)".
DUCT_DEVID_IDENTITY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notarization — nothing to fill in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notarizing is what lets Gatekeeper open a download that did not come from
# the App Store, so it is required precisely *because* Duct is not distributed
# there. It needs credentials, and Apple accepts either an Apple ID with an
# app-specific password, or an App Store Connect API key.
#
# This repository uses the API key: DUCT_ASC_API_KEY_ID, DUCT_ASC_API_ISSUER_ID
# and DUCT_ASC_API_KEY_P8 have been repo secrets since the TestFlight channel
# and are read straight from GitHub. The name is Apple's and it misleads — an
# "App Store Connect API key" is a developer API credential, not an App Store
# submission token, and nothing in the release workflow uploads anything to
# App Store Connect.
#
# So there is no DUCT_NOTARY_* here. Adding an app-specific password would mean
# a second long-lived credential granting notarization under this team, tied to
# one person's Apple ID sign-in, to replace one that already works.
